     EXPLANATION OF OPTION
 
     Use this option to create a logical unit from either spare drives 
     or drives in an existing drive group.
 
 
 
     DEFINITIONS
 
     SPARE DRIVE:  A "spare" drive is a drive in the array subsystem 
     or integrated array that has not yet been configured into a 
     logical unit.  For more information about spare drives, select 
     the "Overview ฤ Modifying/Displaying Drive Statuses" option from 
     the "Help" menu in the drive window.
 
 
     DRIVE GROUP:  A "drive group" is comprised of drives that have 
     been configured into one or more logical units.  All the logical 
     units in a drive group must have the same RAID level.  A logical 
     unit can NOT be in more than one drive group.
 
 
                                (CONTINUED)
     SUMMARY ฤ HOW TO CREATE A LOGICAL UNIT FROM SPARE DRIVES  
 
     NOTE:  A procedure for creating a logical unit from spare drives 
     is included in the help screens following the summaries.
 
     When you create a logical unit from spare drives, you specify the 
     following in order:
 
          ฤ RAID level
          ฤ Channel and ID of each spare drive 
          ฤ Values for the logical unit parameters 
 
     The logical unit parameters include:
 
          ฤ RAID level 
          ฤ Logical Block Size (bytes)
          ฤ Logical Unit Size (mb)
          ฤ Segment Size (blocks)
          ฤ Segment Zero Size (blocks)
          ฤ Delay Interval (tenth's of a second)
          ฤ Blocks Per Delay Interval 
 
                                (CONTINUED)
     NOTE:  The Array Configuration Editor gives you default 
            values for the logical unit parameters.  You may modify
            any value in the logical unit parameter display EXCEPT
            for RAID level.  To modify the RAID level of a logical
            unit, you must delete the logical unit and recreate it.
 
            See the User Guide for the acceptable logical unit 
            parameter values, and the steps to change the RAID level
            or drive grouping.
 
 
     After you create the logical unit, the Array Configuration Editor 
     automatically formats the logical unit.   When you quit ace, 
     sysadm prompts you to run mkdtab or mktable (depending on the 
     version of UNIX V.4 you are using).
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
                                (CONTINUED)
     SUMMARY ฤ HOW TO CREATE A LOGICAL UNIT 
     FROM SPACE IN AN EXISTING DRIVE GROUP
 
     NOTE:  A procedure for creating a logical unit from space in an 
            existing drive group is included in the help screens 
            following the summary.  Remember that you should only 
            create more than one logical unit in an existing drive 
            group if creating a logical unit would exceed your 
            system's capacity.  For more information, see the User 
            Guide.
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
                                (CONTINUED)
     When you select the space remaining row to create a logical unit 
     from an existing drive group, ace automatically assigns to the 
     new logical unit all the drives in that drive group, and the same 
     RAID level that was used for the other logical unit(s) configured 
     in that drive group.
 
     You must specify the following:
 
          ฤ Values for the logical unit parameters 
 
 
     The logical unit parameters include:
 
          ฤ RAID level 
          ฤ Logical Block Size (bytes)
          ฤ Logical Unit Size (mb)
          ฤ Segment Size (blocks)
          ฤ Segment Zero Size (blocks)
          ฤ Delay Interval (tenth's of a second)
          ฤ Blocks Per Delay Interval 
 
 
                                (CONTINUED)
     NOTE:  The Array Configuration Editor gives you default 
            values for the logical unit parameters.  You may modify
            any value in the logical unit parameter display EXCEPT
            for RAID level.  To modify the RAID level of a logical
            unit, you must delete the logical unit and recreate it.
 
            See the User Guide for the acceptable logical unit 
            parameter values, and the required steps to change the 
            RAID level or drive grouping.
 
     After you create the logical unit, the Array Configuration Editor 
     automatically formats the logical unit.  When you quit ace, 
     sysadm prompts you to run mkdtab or mktable (depending on the 
     version of UNIX V.4 you are using).
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
                                (CONTINUED)
     PROCEDURE -- CREATING A LOGICAL UNIT FROM SPARE DRIVES
 
     1.   Select Drive Group 0 with the arrow keys.
 
     2.   Press "c" to create the logical unit.
 
     3.   Specify the RAID level choice.
 
     4.   Enter the Channel and ID of each drive you want in the
          logical unit using the guidelines given in the next 
          help screen.
 
     NOTES:  
           ฤ The drives you can configure into a logical unit are 
             marked with the status "2" in the drive matrix (see
             the drive window).
 
           ฤ It is recommended that you use the maximum number
             of drives allowed when you create a logical unit.
 
 
 
                                (CONTINUED)
