     5.   Press <Return> after entering the drive ID number for the
          last drive you want in the logical unit.
 
     6.   At the prompt, press "y" if you want to save the logical 
          unit parameter defaults for this logical unit, press "n" 
          if you want to modify a value, or press "q" to quit the 
          logical unit creation.
 
      Did you press "y" to accept the values? 
 
       YES     
       Go to step 10, and complete the procedure. ฤฤ>
 
       NO
       Go to step 7, and complete the procedure. ฤฤ>
 
     NOTE:  Press the <Downฤarrow> or <Return> to move the cursor to 
            each logical unit parameter value that can be modified.
 
     7.   Enter a desired value for the parameter, or select a 
          parameter value by pressing the <Downฤarrow> or <Return>.
 
                                (CONTINUED)
     8.   Repeat step 7 until you have modified all the values 
          you want to modify.
 
     9.   At the prompt, press "y" to save all the logical 
          unit parameter defaults for this logical unit, press "n" 
          if you want to modify a value, or press "q" to quit the 
          logical unit creation.
 
      Did you enter "y" to accept the values? 
 
       YES     
       Go to step 10, and complete the procedure. ฤฤ>
 
       NO
       Go back to step 7, and complete the procedure. ฤฤ>
 
     10.  Press <Return>, and the array controller automatically
          formats the logical unit.
 
     11.  Press "q" to quit ace.
 
 
                                (CONTINUED)
     12.  Press <F3> (CONT) to run mkdtab or mktable.
 
          This will take some time.
 
     13.  Add the logical unit as a device by partitioning and 
          slicing it, and creating a filesystem(s) on the slice(s)
          if you want filesystems on the logical unit.
 
 
 
 
     PROCEDURE -- CREATING A LOGICAL UNIT FROM SPACE 
     IN AN EXISTING DRIVE GROUP
 
     1.   With the arrow keys, select the space remaining row for 
          the desired drive group.
 
     2.   Press "c" to create the logical unit.
 
     NOTE:  After you select the "Create Logical Unit" option, you
            are prompted with "Are the values shown OK?"
 
                                (CONTINUED)
     3.   At the prompt, press "y" to save all the logical 
          unit parameter defaults for this logical unit, press "n" 
          if you want to modify a value, or press "q" to quit the 
          logical unit creation.
 
      Did you press "y" to accept the values? 
 
       YES     
       Go to step 7, and complete the procedure. ฤฤ>
 
       NO
       Go to step 4, and complete the procedure. ฤฤ>
 
     NOTE:  Press the <Downฤarrow> or <Return> to move the cursor to 
            each logical unit parameter value that can be modified.
 
     4.   Enter the desired value for the parameter, or select a 
          parameter value by pressing the <Downฤarrow> or <Return>.
 
     5.   Repeat step 4 until you have modified all the values 
          you want to modify.
 
                                (CONTINUED)
     6.   At the prompt, press "y" to save all the logical 
          unit parameter defaults for this logical unit, press "n" 
          if you want to modify a value, or press "q" to quit 
          creating the logical unit.
 
 
      Did you enter "y" to accept the values? 
 
       YES     
       Go to step 7 and complete the procedure. ฤฤ>
 
       NO
       Go back to step 4, and complete the procedure. ฤฤ>
 
     7.   Press <Return>, and the array controller automatically
          formats the logical unit.
 
     8.   Press "q" to quit ace.
 
     9.   Press <F3> (CONT) to run mkdtab or mktable.
 
          This will take some time.
                                (CONTINUED)
     10.  Add the logical unit as a device by partitioning and 
          slicing it, and creating a filesystem(s) on the slice(s)
          if you want filesystems on the logical unit.
 
 
